    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the rule:\nThe most recently pushed item is removed first.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass Stack<T> {\n  private readonly items: T[] = [];\n\n  push(value: T): void {\n    this.items[this.items.length] = value;\n  }\n\n  pop(): T | undefined {\n    if (this.items.length === 0) return undefined;\n\n    const lastIndex = this.items.length - 1;\n    const value = this.items[lastIndex];\n\n    this.items.length = lastIndex;\n    return value;\n  }\n\n  peek(): T | undefined {\n    return this.items.length === 0\n      ? undefined\n      : this.items[this.items.length - 1];\n  }\n\n  isEmpty(): boolean {\n    return this.items.length === 0;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction reverseUsingBuiltInStack(value: string): string {\n  const stack: string[] = [];\n\n  for (const ch of value) stack.push(ch);\n\n  let result = \"\";\n  while (stack.length > 0) {\n    result += stack.pop()!;\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\npush(10), push(20), push(30)\nstack top = 30\npop() → 30\npop() → 20\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\npush/pop/peek → O(1) amortized.\nSpace → O(n).\n\nStep 4 — Interview rule:\nUse a stack when the newest unresolved item must be handled first.",
