    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Maintain two stacks.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass MinStack {\n  private readonly values: number[] = [];\n  private readonly minimums: number[] = [];\n\n  push(value: number): void {\n    this.values.push(value);\n\n    const currentMin =\n      this.minimums.length === 0\n        ? value\n        : Math.min(value, this.minimums[this.minimums.length - 1]!);\n\n    this.minimums.push(currentMin);\n  }\n\n  pop(): number | undefined {\n    if (this.values.length === 0) return undefined;\n\n    this.minimums.pop();\n    return this.values.pop();\n  }\n\n  top(): number | undefined {\n    return this.values[this.values.length - 1];\n  }\n\n  getMin(): number | undefined {\n    return this.minimums[this.minimums.length - 1];\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-friendly:\n```ts\nfunction minValueUsingBuiltIns(values: readonly number[]): number | undefined {\n  if (values.length === 0) return undefined;\n  return Math.min(...values);\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\npush(5) → min=5\npush(2) → min=2\npush(4) → min=2\ngetMin() → 2\npop() → 4, min remains 2.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\npush/pop/top/getMin → O(1).\nSpace → O(n).\n\nStep 4 — Key invariant:\n`minimums[i]` stores the minimum value among `values[0..i]`.",
