    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Identify the monotonic rule:\nFor a next-greater-style problem, keep unresolved values in decreasing order.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction nextGreaterIndices(nums: readonly number[]): number[] {\n  const result = new Array<number>(nums.length).fill(-1);\n  const stack: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    while (\n      stack.length > 0 &&\n      nums[stack[stack.length - 1]!] < nums[i]!\n    ) {\n      const index = stack.pop()!;\n      result[index] = nums[i]!;\n    }\n\n    stack.push(i);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN Map-free alternative:\n```ts\nfunction nextGreaterValuesUsingBuiltIns(\n  nums: readonly number[],\n): number[] {\n  const result: number[] = new Array(nums.length).fill(-1);\n  const stack: number[] = [];\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    while (stack.length && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]!] < nums[i]!) {\n      result[stack.pop()!] = nums[i]!;\n    }\n    stack.push(i);\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n[2,1,2,4,3]\n2 waits\n1 waits\n2 resolves 1 → 2\n4 resolves 2 and 2 → 4\n3 resolves nothing\nresult [4,2,4,-1,-1].\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nO(n) time because each index enters and leaves the stack at most once.\nO(n) space.\n\nStep 4 — Interview proof:\nThe stack contains only unresolved indices that still have no suitable greater value to their right.",
