    category: "Stack",
    expectedAnswer: "Expression evaluation uses stacks to separate operands and operators while respecting precedence and parentheses. For a simple calculator without unary operators or exponentiation, one stack-based approach is to tokenize, apply higher-precedence operations first, then handle addition/subtraction.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Define the grammar before coding. This example supports non-negative integers, +, -, *, / and spaces.\n\nWITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nfunction evaluateExpression(value: string): number {\n  const values: number[] = [];\n  const operators: string[] = [];\n\n  const precedence = (operator: string): number =>\n    operator === \"+\" || operator === \"-\" ? 1 : 2;\n\n  const applyTop = (): void => {\n    const operator = operators.pop()!;\n    const right = values.pop()!;\n    const left = values.pop()!;\n\n    if (operator === \"+\") values.push(left + right);\n    else if (operator === \"-\") values.push(left - right);\n    else if (operator === \"*\") values.push(left * right);\n    else values.push(Math.trunc(left / right));\n  };\n\n  let i = 0;\n\n  while (i < value.length) {\n    const ch = value[i]!;\n\n    if (ch === \" \") {\n      i++;\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    if (ch >= \"0\" && ch <= \"9\") {\n      let number = 0;\n\n      while (\n        i < value.length &&\n        value[i]! >= \"0\" &&\n        value[i]! <= \"9\"\n      ) {\n        number = number * 10 + (value.charCodeAt(i) - 48);\n        i++;\n      }\n\n      values.push(number);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    while (\n      operators.length > 0 &&\n      precedence(operators[operators.length - 1]!) >= precedence(ch)\n    ) {\n      applyTop();\n    }\n\n    operators.push(ch);\n    i++;\n  }\n\n  while (operators.length > 0) {\n    applyTop();\n  }\n\n  return values[0] ?? 0;\n}\n```\n\nWITH BUILT-IN-style helper:\n```ts\nfunction evaluateExpressionUsingBuiltIns(value: string): number {\n  // For controlled/trusted input only. `Function` is NOT safe for untrusted text.\n  const tokens = value.match(/\d+|[-+*/]/g) ?? [];\n  if (tokens.length === 0) return 0;\n\n  const normalized = tokens.join(\" \");\n  return Number(new Function(`return (${normalized})`)());\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — Example:\n`2 + 3 * 4`\nmultiplication first → 12\nthen 2 + 12 → 14.\n\nStep 3 — Complexity:\nManual parser O(n) time and O(n) stack/token state.\n\nStep 4 — Production note:\nNever evaluate untrusted user input with `eval`/`Function`; use a real parser or a safe expression grammar.",
